# 🔬 Automated AFM Data Acquisition & Processing Pipeline

![Python](https://img.shields.io/badge/Python-3.8+-blue.svg?style=for-the-badge&logo=python&logoColor=white)
![TensorFlow](https://img.shields.io/badge/TensorFlow-2.x-FF6F00.svg?style=for-the-badge&logo=tensorflow&logoColor=white)
![Scikit-Image](https://img.shields.io/badge/Scikit--Image-Image%20Processing-lightgrey.svg?style=for-the-badge)
![NumPy](https://img.shields.io/badge/NumPy-Data%20Math-013243.svg?style=for-the-badge&logo=numpy&logoColor=white)
![Status](https://img.shields.io/badge/Status-Production_Ready-success.svg?style=for-the-badge)

An automated computer vision and deep learning pipeline designed to process raw Atomic Force Microscopy (AFM) data, mathematically correct physical scan artifacts at the nanoscale, and utilize a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) to read microscopic etched navigation markers.

---

## 📖 Overview

Atomic Force Microscopy (AFM) generates high-resolution nanoscale topography maps. However, raw AFM data is frequently plagued by system-induced physical artifacts, such as sample tilt (non-orthogonal tip approach) and thermal/piezo drift (horizontal stripe noise). 

This project completely automates the evaluation pipeline:
1. **Data Parsing:** Handles both proprietary `.safm` files and simulated `.npy` matrices.
2. **Nanoscale Scan Correction:** Employs Masked Plane and Line Correction algorithms to mathematically flatten the substrate *without* distorting the physical structural data.
3. **Morphological Segmentation:** Uses Otsu thresholding and morphological operations (erosion/dilation) to isolate micro-etched navigation markers.
4. **Deep Learning Inference:** Preprocesses extracted digits (aspect-ratio scaling, Center-of-Mass alignment) and classifies them using a custom CNN trained on the MNIST dataset.

---

## 🔒 Data Privacy & Synthetic AFM Generation (Overcoming NDA)

Due to Non-Disclosure Agreements (NDA) regarding the physical samples used in the laboratory, the raw proprietary `.safm` files cannot be published in this public repository. 

To make this project fully reproducible, I engineered a **physics-based AFM scan synthesizer** (`scripts/generate_synthetic_afm.py`). This generator simulates:
* **Sample Tilt:** Global gradient addition simulating a non-orthogonal tip approach.
* **Piezo Actuator Artifacts:** Row-by-row offset noise mimicking physical scanning errors.
* **Tip Convolution:** Gaussian blurring to simulate the finite radius of the physical AFM tip.
* **Structural Etching:** Uses actual MNIST digits arrayed around a navigational cross-marker, subjected to random physical rotations and mirroring.

The pipeline's custom parser seamlessly handles both the internal `.safm` format and the public synthetic `.npy` format.

---

## 🖼️ Pipeline Showcase

### 1. Masked Plane & Line Correction
Raw high-resolution scans exhibit extreme global tilt and piezo line artifacts. By computing a gradient-based background mask, we fit a correction plane *only to the flat substrate*, resulting in a perfectly flattened topography that preserves the etched numbers.
<p align="center">
  <!-- REPLACE THE SRC LINK WITH YOUR ACTUAL IMAGE PATH ONCE UPLOADED -->
  <img src="docs/images/scan_correction.png" width="800" alt="Masked Scan Correction"/>
</p>

### 2. Feature Extraction & Alignment
The digits are extracted using bounding-box filtering to ignore oversized structures (like the central navigation cross). The image is programmatically rotated and mirrored based on the detected orientation of the marker.
<p align="center">
  <!-- REPLACE THE SRC LINK WITH YOUR ACTUAL IMAGE PATH ONCE UPLOADED -->
  <img src="docs/images/extraction.png" width="800" alt="Feature Extraction"/>
</p>

### 3. CNN Digit Recognition & Center-of-Mass Fix
The digits are isolated, blurred to mimic MNIST stroke gradients, and centered by their **Center of Mass** (a critical step for bridging the gap between AFM data and the MNIST distribution, preventing issues like confusing a '2' with a '5'). The CNN then classifies the sequence with high confidence.
<p align="center">
  <!-- REPLACE THE SRC LINK WITH YOUR ACTUAL IMAGE PATH ONCE UPLOADED -->
  <img src="docs/images/cnn_results.png" width="800" alt="CNN Probabilities"/>
</p>

